# Line endings belong to the repository, not to the machine that checked it
# out. Without this file a Windows clone with core.autocrlf=true - which the
# capture machine has - writes CRLF into the working tree, and every tool
# that rewrites a file writes LF back. git then reports a modification whose
# content is byte-identical, `git pull --ff-only` refuses to overwrite it,
# and the capture machine silently stops receiving commits. That is not
# hypothetical: on 2026-10-27 it left the machine four commits behind, the
# implementation list read there was stale, and an item the repository had
# already finished was executed a second time.
#
# The declaration is `eol=lf`, not a request to configure core.autocrlf on
# each machine. A rule that lives in the repository holds for every clone
# including the next one; a rule that lives in a local config holds until
# someone clones again.
* text=auto eol=lf

# The mutation log is gzip, and it is the source of truth for the spec graph:
# spec.db is a local cache rebuilt from these files. git's own heuristic
# already classifies them as binary - `git ls-files --eol` reports i/-text -
# but heuristics are a poor guard for the one artifact whose corruption
# cannot be noticed by reading it. Declared, not detected.
*.cbm binary
*.cbs binary
